\title[The Real-Model Knee Collapses and Saturates]{The Real-Model Knee Collapses and Saturates:\\ Depth Sandwiches, Context Monotonicity, and Selection Gaps for Top-$k$ Attention}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the \emph{lossless attention knee}: the smallest number $\ks$ of highest-weighted keys per query that a transformer may keep, in every layer, while still retaining at least $98\%$ of its full-attention next-token accuracy. We measured it on a pretrained 24-layer, 0.5-billion-parameter language model with grouped-query attention, in full precision on held-out encyclopedic text. At context lengths $512$, $1024$, $2048$ the knee is $\ks=16,32,24$. That is $24$, $24$ and $64$ times below the law $\ks\approx d\cdot\ctx/32$ (with $d$ the depth) that had described a long series of synthetic transformers. Random selection at matched budget loses $68$--$82$ accuracy points, an order of magnitude more than in the toy family. We prove three structural results. (i) A \emph{depth sandwich}: for per-layer deficits $\delta_l\in[0,1]$ with total $D$, the compounded retention satisfies $1-D\le\prod_l(1-\delta_l)\le 1/(1+D)$. Hence the toy knee is linear in depth, and it collapses to a depth-independent bound when only $m$ layers have heavy tails. (ii) \emph{Context monotonicity and saturation}: for a fixed positive profile the knee is non-decreasing in context, and it is eventually constant under geometric decay. The measured grid does not certify a decline. (iii) \emph{Selection gaps}: the top-$k$ share is at least $k/n$, band-limited profiles have gap at most $k(M-c)/(nc)$, and a knee forces a gap of at least $g-\ks/n$, which is $>0.968$ at the operating point. A Cauchy--Schwarz bound converts the measured effective supports into mass statements: any $24$-key truncation of the most diffuse layer keeps less than $43.3\%$ of its mass.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Autoregressive transformers store, for every past position, a key and a value vector in every layer (the \emph{KV cache}). Generating each new token requires reading the whole cache, so long-context decoding is dominated by memory traffic. Much engineering work, including sparse attention, heavy-hitter retention, attention-sink windows and score-based cache compression, exploits the fact that attention is concentrated on few keys. Two quantitative questions remain: how few keys suffice under a fixed, strict notion of ``lossless'', and how that number scales with depth and context.

An earlier programme of 48 controlled experiments on small synthetic transformers measured the knee under a fixed protocol: a $0.98$ accuracy-retention gate, oracle top-$k$ selection from the model's own attention scores, and held-out evaluation. It found an approximately bilinear law
\[
  \ks_{\mathrm{toy}}\approx\frac{d\cdot\ctx}{32}.
\]
This paper reports the same protocol on a real pretrained model and develops the mathematics explaining why the toy law fails in exactly the observed way.

\subsection{Measurements}

The model is a 24-layer, 0.5B-parameter pretrained transformer with grouped-query attention (two KV heads) and a vocabulary of about 151k tokens. Evaluation uses the last $10\%$ of wikitext-103, with zero training. All computation is in 32-bit floating point. Before measurement the custom top-$k$ forward pass was validated against the reference eager implementation, with maximum absolute logit difference $0.0000$. The evaluation is deterministic: a re-run reproduced the baseline accuracy $0.4787$ and loss $2.6355$ exactly.

