\emph{Baselines.} Random-$k$ selection at matched budget is worse than oracle top-$k$ by $+82.0$, $+71.8$, $+81.9$, $+70.0$, $+79.9$, $+68.0$ accuracy points. Across all toy experiments the gap had ranged over only $+1.7$ to $+11.7$. Local-window selection is worse by $40$--$55$ points. At ctx $2048$ a $256$-key local window retains only $0.598$, while oracle top-$32$ retains $0.9867$.

\emph{Depth map.} With effective support $\Neff=1/\sum_ip_i^2$, the median layer has $\Neff\approx10$--$12$, independent of context from $512$ to $2048$. (The toy family grew from $46$ to $526$.) The minimum is about $2.9$ keys. Only the last two layers, L22 and L23 (counting from zero), are diffuse, with $\Neff$ equal to $51\to83\to128.5$ and $33\to50\to72$. Even L22 at $2048$ is $3.9\times$ less diffuse than the mean toy layer.

\emph{Practical reading.} At ctx $2048$, an oracle working set of $24$ out of $2048$ rows gives $85\times$ fewer KV reads, reported as a $64\times$ reduction in KV bytes per sequence. The oracle-to-policy gap is left open.

\subsection{What needs explaining}
(A) The ratio $1/24=1/d$: the depth multiplier is absent. (B) The knee doubles from $512$ to $1024$ but not from $1024$ to $2048$. (C) Selection matters an order of magnitude more than in the toy family.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $g\in(0,1)$ is the gate; in applications $g=\tau=0.98$.

\begin{definition}[Profile, head mass, retained fraction]
A \emph{profile} is a sequence $w=(w_0,w_1,\dots)$ of positive reals. It is \emph{sorted} if $w_{i+1}\le w_i$ for all $i$. Write $H(n)=\sum_{i<n}w_i$. For $n\ge1$ and $k\ge0$ define
\[
  \rho(n,k)=\frac{H(\min(k,n))}{H(n)}\in[0,1].
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Knee]
For $\tau\le1$, $\ks(n,\tau)=\min\{k\in\mathbb N:\rho(n,k)\ge\tau\}$. It is well defined since $\rho(n,n)=1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Deficits and compounded retention]
A stack of layers indexed by a finite set $S$ has deficits $\delta_l\in[0,1]$, compounded retention $R=\prod_{l\in S}(1-\delta_l)$ and total deficit $D=\sum_{l\in S}\delta_l$. If $\delta_l=\delta_l(k)$ depends on a budget, the depth-compounded knee is $\ks_{\mathrm{depth}}=\min\{k:R(k)\ge g\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Toy tail deficit]
For $c>0$, $\delta(k)=c/(c+k)\in(0,1]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Selection gap]
$\Gamma(n,k)=\rho(n,k)-k/n$. Here $k/n$ is the expected mass fraction captured by a uniformly random $k$-subset of the first $n$ keys.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Collision mass, effective support]
For $p\ge0$ on a finite set $S$, $C(p)=\sum_{i\in S}p_i^2$, and $\Neff(p)=1/C(p)$ when $C(p)>0$.
\end{definition}

\section{Depth: the sandwich, the toy multiplier, and its collapse}

\begin{theorem}[Weierstrass lower bound]\label{thm:weier}
If $\delta_l\in[0,1]$ for all $l\in S$, then $1-D\le R$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $S$. If $P\ge1-\Sigma$ with $\Sigma\ge0$ and $\delta\in[0,1]$, then $(1-\delta)P\ge(1-\delta)(1-\Sigma)=1-\delta-\Sigma+\delta\Sigma\ge1-(\delta+\Sigma)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:unit}
Under the same hypotheses, $0\le R\le1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Upper bound]\label{thm:upper}
If $\delta_l\in[0,1]$ for all $l$, then $R(1+D)\le1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $S$. With $P,\Sigma$ for the smaller stack,
$(1-\delta)P(1+\delta+\Sigma)=(1-\delta)P(1+\Sigma)+\delta(1-\delta)P$.
By induction the first term is at most $1-\delta$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:unit} the second is at most $\delta$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Depth sandwich]
For deficits in $[0,1]$,
\[
  1-D\;\le\;\prod_{l\in S}(1-\delta_l)\;\le\;\frac{1}{1+D}.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[The gate constrains the total deficit]\label{cor:gate}
(i) If $D\le1-g$ then $R\ge g$. (ii) If $R\ge g$ then $gD\le1-g$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
(i) follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:weier}. For (ii), multiply $g\le R$ by $1+D\ge0$ and use Theorem~\ref{thm:upper}.
\end{proof}

At $g=0.98$ the two conditions read $D\le0.0200$ and $D\le0.0204$. Passing the gate is, up to the factor $g$, the same as having total deficit at most $1-g$.

\begin{theorem}[Toy knee is linear in depth]\label{thm:toy}
If all $d=|S|$ layers carry $\delta(k)=c/(c+k)$ and $0<g<1$, then every passing $k$ satisfies $gdc/(1-g)-c\le k$, and every $k\ge dc/(1-g)$ passes. Hence
\[
  \frac{gdc}{1-g}-c\;\le\;\ks_{\mathrm{depth}}\;\le\;\Big\lceil\frac{dc}{1-g}\Big\rceil.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$D(k)=dc/(c+k)$. Corollary~\ref{cor:gate}(ii) gives $gdc\le(1-g)(c+k)$. Conversely, $k\ge dc/(1-g)$ gives $D(k)\le1-g$, and Corollary~\ref{cor:gate}(i) applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Depth-multiplier collapse]\label{thm:collapse}
Let $T\subseteq S$ with $|T|=m$. Assume $\delta_l(k)\in[0,1]$ for all $l,k$; $\delta_l(k)\le c/(c+k)$ for $l\in T$; and $\delta_l(k)=0$ for $l\notin T$, $k\ge k_0$. Then
\[
  \ks_{\mathrm{depth}}\;\le\;\max\Big(k_0,\Big\lceil\frac{mc}{1-g}\Big\rceil\Big),
\]
uniformly in the depth $|S|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
At the stated budget $K$, the layers outside $T$ contribute nothing, so $D(K)\le mc/(c+K)\le1-g$ because $K\ge mc/(1-g)$. Apply Corollary~\ref{cor:gate}(i).
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The measured map supplies the hypotheses. The median layer has $\Neff\approx10$--$12$, independent of context, and only $m=2$ layers are diffuse. The multiplier therefore drops from $d=24$ to $m=2$, which is observation (A). If $c$ does not grow with context, the bound is context-free as well, consistent with (B).
\end{remark}

\section{Context: monotonicity, brackets, and saturation}

Fix a positive profile $w$.

\begin{theorem}[Retained mass is antitone in context]\label{thm:anti}
If $0<n\le m$ then $\rho(m,k)\le\rho(n,k)$ for every $k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $k\le n$ the numerators agree and $H(n)\le H(m)$. For $k>n$, $\rho(n,k)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotone knee]\label{thm:mono}
If $0<n\le m$ and $\tau\le1$, then $\ks(n,\tau)\le\ks(m,\tau)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The knee budget at $m$ passes at $m$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:anti} it also passes at $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[A declining knee forces a change of profile]
If positive profiles $w,w'$ and $n\le m$ satisfy $\ks_{w'}(m,\tau)<\ks_w(n,\tau)$, then $w'\neq w$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[The measured brackets]\label{prop:brackets}
Let $\mathrm{ret}_1,\mathrm{ret}_2$ be the retained-accuracy curves at contexts $1024$ and $2048$. Suppose each is threshold-monotone, i.e.\ $\mathrm{ret}_i(k)\ge0.98\iff k\ge k_i$. If $\mathrm{ret}_1(16)<0.98\le\mathrm{ret}_1(32)$ and $\mathrm{ret}_2(16)<0.98\le\mathrm{ret}_2(24)$, then $k_1\in(16,32]$, $k_2\in(16,24]$, and these intervals overlap.
\end{proposition}

Thus the reported step $32\to24$ is not certified. The data are consistent with a flat plateau at $24$.

\begin{theorem}[Uniform bound under geometric decay]\label{thm:unif}
If $w_{i+1}\le rw_i$ for some $0<r<1$ and $\tau<1$, then there is $B$ with $\ks(n,\tau)\le B$ for all $n\ge1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $k<n$, $\sum_{k\le i<n}w_i\le w_0r^k/(1-r)$ and $H(n)\ge w_0$, so $1-\rho(n,k)\le r^k/(1-r)$. Choose $B$ with $r^B/(1-r)\le1-\tau$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Saturation]
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:unif}, there exist $K,N$ with $\ks(n,\tau)=K$ for all $n\ge N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The sequence is non-decreasing (Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}) and bounded (Theorem~\ref{thm:unif}). A non-decreasing bounded sequence of natural numbers attains its supremum and stays there.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
For $w_i=(i+1)^{-1.1}$ and $\tau=0.98$, the knee is about $422$, $821$, $1594$ at $n=512,1024,2048$: unbounded growth. For $w_i=0.8^i$ it is $18$ for all $n\ge64$. A non-saturating knee in a larger model would therefore refute geometric decay of its sorted attention profile.
\end{remark}

\section{Selection gaps}

\begin{lemma}[Chebyshev prefix inequality]
If $w$ is sorted and nonnegative and $k\le n$, then $kH(n)\le nH(k)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $n\ge k$. Since $w_i\ge w_n$ for $i<k$, we have $kw_n\le H(k)$, so $kH(n+1)\le nH(k)+H(k)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Top-$k$ beats uniform]
If $w$ is positive and sorted, $n\ge1$ and $k\le n$, then $\rho(n,k)\ge k/n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Band profiles have small gaps]\label{thm:band}
If $0<c\le w_i\le M$ for all $i$ and $k\le n$, then $\Gamma(n,k)\le k(M-c)/(nc)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$H(n)\ge nc$ and $H(k)\le kM$, so $\rho(n,k)\le kM/(nc)$. Subtract $k/n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Gap at a knee]
For a positive profile and $\tau\le1$, $\Gamma(n,\ks(n,\tau))\ge\tau-\ks(n,\tau)/n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Operating point]
If $\ks(2048,0.98)=24$, then $\Gamma(2048,24)>0.968$. Every positive profile with $c\le w_i\le M\le2c$ has $\Gamma(2048,24)<0.012$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
$0.98-24/2048=0.96828\ldots$. For the band case, Theorem~\ref{thm:band} gives $\Gamma\le24/2048=0.01171\ldots$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
These statements are in mass units, whereas the measured gaps are in accuracy, so they explain the \emph{shape} of observation (C). A small knee at long context \emph{is} a large selection gap. A profile flat enough for random selection to compete cannot have a small knee.
\end{remark}

\section{The depth map in mass units}

\begin{lemma}[Cauchy--Schwarz]
For $p\ge0$ on $S$ and $T\subseteq S$, $(\sum_{i\in T}p_i)^2\le|T|\,C(p)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}
If $\Neff(p)=N>0$ and $|T|\le k$, then $(\sum_{i\in T}p_i)^2\le k/N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Diffuse layer at the knee]
If $\Neff=128.5$ (L22 at ctx $2048$), any set of at most $24$ keys carries less than $43.3\%$ of the mass, since $\sqrt{24/128.5}=0.43217\ldots$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Median-layer mass knee]
If $\Neff=12$, any key set carrying at least $98\%$ of the mass has at least $12$ elements, since $|T|\ge0.98^2\cdot12=11.52\ldots$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
The mass knee of a typical layer is at least $12$ and context-free, the same order as the measured accuracy knees. At the accuracy knee, L22 loses more than half its mass, yet accuracy is retained. Mass knees and accuracy knees therefore separate in the tail layers.
\end{remark}

\section{Discussion}

The sandwich is unconditional and makes the gate a constraint on a sum. Any model whose deficits concentrate in a few layers therefore has a depth-independent knee; the toy multiplier is the special case of uniformly heavy tails. Monotonicity is unconditional for a fixed profile, saturation follows from geometric decay, and a strict decline needs a context-dependent profile, which the present grid does not certify. The selection-gap results link knee size to random-selection loss with no model-specific input. The empirical content is that a real model satisfies the concentrated-majority hypothesis.

Heavy-hitter retention, sink-plus-window caches and score-based compression are established techniques. The new content is a set of measured laws under a fixed strict protocol: the collapse of the depth multiplier, the plateau shape, the tenfold inflation of selection importance, and the depth map. The mathematics shows how these laws fit together.

\emph{Limitations.} The study covers one model, one size and one corpus. Two knees pass the gate by about half a standard error. The bracket $(16,32]$ at ctx $1024$ is unpinned. Selection is by an oracle, not a policy. The selection-gap and depth-map results are stated in mass units.

\section{Future work}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \emph{Two-layer tail additivity}: pruning only L22 and L23 should reproduce the full-model knee within one grid step.
\item \emph{Accuracy--mass separation}: test whether per-token logit margins absorb the dropped tail of diffuse layers.
\item \emph{Size-invariant plateau}: for larger models of the same family, test whether $\ks(\ctx)$ saturates at a plateau of at most about $32$ by ctx $2048$. A non-saturating knee refutes geometric decay.
\item \emph{Oracle-to-policy gap}: compare online accumulated-score eviction against the oracle bound.
\item \emph{Corpus robustness and quantization floors} on the same protocol.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
On a real pretrained transformer the lossless attention knee is $16$--$32$ keys and roughly flat in context, $24$--$64$ times below the synthetic law. Three facts explain this. Depth enters only through how many layers have heavy tails. A fixed profile can only saturate. A small knee and a large selection gap are one phenomenon.

